Bug summary: In Joda-Time 2.0, constructing a DateTime with a local time that is ambiguous (DST overlap) in Europe/London resolves to the wrong offset. The reporter expects the earlier (summer-time, +01:00) instant, but the result is the later (standard-time, Z) one, so the three zone times no longer represent the same instant.

Concrete examples:
[{'input': 'new DateTime(2011, 10, 30, 1, 59, 0, 0, DateTimeZone.forID("Europe/London"))', 'expected': '2011-10-30T01:59:00.000+01:00 (earlier instant, summer time, during the overlap)', 'actual': '2011-10-30T01:59:00.000Z (later instant, standard time)'}, {'input': 'new DateTime(2011, 10, 30, 2, 59, 0, 0, DateTimeZone.forID("Europe/Oslo"))', 'expected': '2011-10-30T02:59:00.000+02:00 (earlier instant, summer time)', 'actual': "2011-10-30T02:59:00.000+02:00 in 2.0 (the reporter's 1.6.2 output was +01:00)"}, {'input': 'new DateTime(2011, 10, 30, 3, 59, 0, 0, DateTimeZone.forID("Europe/Helsinki"))', 'expected': '2011-10-30T03:59:00.000+03:00 (earlier instant, summer time)', 'actual': "2011-10-30T03:59:00.000+03:00 in 2.0 (the reporter's 1.6.2 output was +02:00)"}]

